<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/advanced/agroecology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Agroecology is <strong>farming designed as an ecosystem rather than a factory</strong> —
    nutrient cycles, pest regulation and biodiversity as free services the design hires. It is
    agriculture\'s most interesting argument about what a farm IS.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="interactions, tested patterns, the honest debate, the movement" widget="equationStep" config={agroecoCfg} />

  <h2>Design patterns that work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cover crops and rotations rebuild everything:</strong> soil organic matter (the soil lesson), nitrogen (legumes), pest cycles (the IPM lesson) — the cheapest practices in agronomy, repeatedly under-used because their payback is systemic, not per-acre this year.</li>
    <li><strong>Integrated systems close loops:</strong> crop-livestock integration (feed from crops, manure to crops), rice-fish-aqua systems in Asia — nutrient imports approach zero; the farm as thermodynamic miser.</li>
    <li><strong>Landscape-scale ecology matters:</strong> hedgerows and field margins host the predators and pollinators (the ecology lesson) — a farm\'s yield depends partly on its neighbours\' edges.</li>
    <li><strong>The honest middle:</strong> most realistic futures mix both paradigms — precision\'s efficiency inside ecological architecture (the systems lesson: optimise the whole, not the doctrine).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Altieri, Agroecology; the push-pull programme (ICIPE); FAO agroecology framework; Phalan et al. (land sparing/sharing debate, 2011).</p>
</TopicPage>
